{
  "title": "Hard Money Loan Calculator for Total Cost and Interest",
  "metaDescription": "Use this hard money loan calculator to add points, fees, interest and extensions. See cash to close, balloon payoff, loan ratios and profit before tax on a flip.",
  "h1": "Hard Money Loan Calculator",
  "introText": "Your total cost is closing points and fees plus interest for every month you hold the loan. The hard money loan calculator shows that cost alongside your loan amount, cash to close, monthly interest-only payment, and balloon payoff. It also returns loan-to-cost and loan-to-after-repair-value percentages. Enter an after-repair value to see profit before tax. Start with the purchase price, rehab budget, and terms from your lender quote. A hard money loan here means a short-term loan with points and fees at closing, interest-only payments, and repayment of the full balance at exit. The figures already in the calculator are example inputs, not lender data; replace them with the terms for the deal you want to calculate.\n\nAt ModernWallet, we ran one example deal through internal modeling and found a $20,900 total financing cost on a $230,000 loan held for 6 months. The example uses a $200,000 purchase and a $50,000 rehab budget. The lender advances 90% of the purchase and 100% of the rehab, with a 12% annual percentage rate (APR). Closing charges are 2 points and $2,500 in other fees. The result includes $13,800 in interest and $7,100 in points and fees. Repayment of the $230,000 loan balance is a separate balloon payoff, so it does not appear as another financing charge. You can reproduce the example with the starting inputs, then enter your own terms to see how the cost changes.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How the Cost Adds Up",
      "body": "Total financing cost adds the points paid at closing, lender and other fees, interest during the months held, and any extension fee. Points equal the loan amount multiplied by the points percentage. For each month, interest equals the outstanding balance multiplied by the APR and divided by 12.\n\n| Piece | Example amount |\n|---|---|\n| Loan | $230,000 |\n| Points | $4,600 |\n| Other fees | $2,500 |\n| Interest-only payment | $2,300 a month |\n| Interest over 6 months | $13,800 |\n| Total financing cost | $20,900 |\n| Balloon payoff | $230,000 |\n| Cash to close | $27,100 |\n\nThe loan combines $180,000 advanced against the purchase with $50,000 advanced for rehab. The $20,000 down payment, $4,600 in points, and $2,500 in other fees make cash to close $27,100. Cash to close describes money needed at closing; it is not the same measure as total financing cost.\n\nInterest-only payments cover interest while the balance remains outstanding. In this example, the $2,300 monthly payment does not pay down the $230,000 loan. The Balloon payoff at exit line therefore shows the full loan balance, separate from the interest and closing charges already counted in financing cost.\n\nSimple annualized cost divides total financing cost by the loan amount and then by the years held. It is 18.17% for this example, which includes points and fees as well as interest. It is not an internal rate of return (IRR): the calculation does not use the dates of individual cash flows to produce an IRR."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Loan to Cost and Loan to ARV Explained",
      "body": "Loan to cost (LTC) divides the loan amount by the purchase price plus the rehab budget. For the example, the $230,000 loan divided by the $200,000 purchase and $50,000 rehab budget gives 92%. This percentage describes the share of those project costs covered by the modeled loan; it does not include financing charges or other holding costs in the denominator.\n\nLoan to after-repair value (ARV) divides the loan amount by the after-repair value you enter. At an example ARV of $330,000, the $230,000 loan produces a loan-to-ARV figure of 69.7%. Leave ARV blank if you only need the loan-cost calculation; the loan-to-ARV and profit figures need an ARV input.\n\nThe profit calculation starts with ARV and subtracts selling costs, purchase price, rehab budget, total financing cost, and other holding costs. At $330,000 ARV and 6% selling costs, the modeled selling costs are $19,800. Other holding costs of $1,000 a month add up to $6,000 over 6 months.\n\nWith those inputs, Profit before tax is $33,300. That figure counts the $20,900 financing cost as an expense, alongside the purchase and rehab costs. The balloon payoff is not subtracted again: the purchase and rehab amounts already represent the project spending financed in part by the loan.\n\nCash to close and profit answer different questions. Cash to close shows the modeled amount needed to complete the purchase with the stated advance percentages, points, and fees. Profit before tax uses the entered sale value and costs to show the modeled outcome at exit."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Moves the Total Most",
      "body": "Holding time changes the example's financing cost by $2,300 for each additional month at the full loan balance. The calculator's What moves the total table also changes points and APR while keeping the other example inputs fixed. These figures show how much each specified change moves the $20,900 starting total.\n\n| Change | Total financing cost | Change from example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hold for 3 months | $14,000 | $6,900 lower |\n| Hold for 9 months | $27,800 | $6,900 higher |\n| Charge 1 point | $18,600 | $2,300 lower |\n| Charge 3 points | $23,200 | $2,300 higher |\n| Use 10% APR | $18,600 | $2,300 lower |\n| Use 14% APR | $23,200 | $2,300 higher |\n\nA point equals 1% of the loan amount. On the $230,000 example loan, changing the charge by one point changes closing cost by $2,300. The interest-rate rows show the same dollar change for the stated APR changes over the example holding period; they do not tell you what a lender will quote.\n\nAn extension can change both interest and fees. On the example loan, a 3-month extension with a 1% extension fee adds $6,900 of interest and a $2,300 fee. That is $9,200 more in financing cost, bringing the total to $30,100. The extension months accrue interest on the full loan balance.\n\nThe rehab funding choice changes when interest begins on the rehab advance. If rehab money is Funded at closing, the full loan accrues interest from the first month. If it is Drawn evenly each month, the modeled rehab balance grows in equal monthly portions; the purchase advance remains outstanding throughout.\n\nFor the example's 6-month hold, even draws reduce interest from $13,800 to $12,550. Total financing cost falls from $20,900 to $19,650. Select the funding method that matches the schedule you are modeling rather than treating the two totals as interchangeable quotes.\n\nTime held is the larger lever in the stated comparison: a 3-month change moves the total by $6,900. A one-point change moves it by $2,300. Different draw timing, extension terms, points, or fees would change the answer, so use the terms for the particular loan you are comparing.\n\nThe hard money loan calculator cannot supply a lender quote. If you need a quoted rate or fee, obtain those terms from a lender before using its output to compare financing costs. Enter the terms from your lender quote in the calculator above and read the Total financing cost line."
    }
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "The calculator does not subtract income tax from profit. For the tax issue associated with a short-term flip, read [flipping houses and its tax trap](/guides/real-estate-investment-options/) and keep the displayed profit labeled before tax.",
    "Draw inspection fees are not included automatically. If your terms list them, add their amount to Lender, document and other fees.",
    "Default interest and prepayment minimums are not modeled. Read the repayment terms in the quote before relying on the displayed interest total for a different payoff schedule.",
    "Profit before tax ignores any costs you have not entered, as well as tax. Enter the available selling and holding costs, and treat the result as a calculation based only on those inputs.",
    "The calculator does not compare this loan with a different short-term financing route. Use [bridge loan vs HELOC](/compare/bridge-loan-vs-heloc/) to examine that choice, then enter separate terms in the [bridge loan calculator](/real-estate/bridge-loan-calculator/)."
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How do you calculate loan to cost?",
      "answer": "Divide the loan amount by the purchase price plus the rehab budget. The example combines a $200,000 purchase and a $50,000 rehab budget. A $230,000 loan against those costs gives a 92% loan-to-cost figure. This calculation uses the stated purchase and rehab costs; it does not add interest or fees to that denominator."
    },
    {
      "question": "Are hard money loans interest only?",
      "answer": "This calculator models interest-only payments each month and repayment of the full balance at exit. Its monthly payment does not reduce the balloon payoff. Your lender's terms determine whether the loan you are considering follows that structure. Use the payment and payoff terms in your quote when interpreting the results."
    },
    {
      "question": "How much do points add to a hard money loan?",
      "answer": "Points add the loan amount multiplied by the points percentage to closing cost. On the $230,000 example loan, 2 points add $4,600. Each point changes that loan's closing cost by $2,300. Points are included in Total financing cost even though they are paid at closing rather than through monthly interest."
    },
